WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:06.410
Choosing a building means planning for years of heat, heavy rain, and changing needs.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.690
But the evidence is scattered across maps, records, and photographs.

3
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.210
This is GEONOS.

4
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:18.700
We bring it together to help people make more climate-resilient building decisions.

5
00:00:19.697 --> 00:00:23.187
Here, we’re exploring a cold-storage retrofit in Brooklyn.

6
00:00:23.217 --> 00:00:29.797
Muse Voice turns a spoken question into an editable message, while the map keeps both buildings in view.

7
00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:38.077
Across New York, search addresses, filter records, and explore mapped layers in 3D.

8
00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:43.037
A second selection opens a same-scale building comparison.

9
00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.040
Muse Spark brings official records into the conversation.

10
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:53.440
In this published 2050 rainfall scenario, mapped deep water reaches the ground at 572 Smith’s outline.

11
00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:58.370
At 592 Smith, the nearest deep-water class is about forty-seven meters away.

12
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.370
That gives us a useful next step: compare entrance and equipment elevations.

13
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.100
A separate footprint requirement helps us see which candidate fits the project’s initial size condition.

14
00:01:10.988 --> 00:01:16.798
Climate Studio combines station history, paired temperature and dew point, and regional future pathways to

15
00:01:16.828 --> 00:01:19.408
frame the project’s operating conditions.

16
00:01:20.405 --> 00:01:27.545
Energy reports trace each reporting boundary’s buildings and floor area, guiding requests for meter schedules.

17
00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:32.900
SAM 3.1 marks roof and tree regions in the dated aerials.

18
00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.940
Compare roof detections with the mapped outlines.

19
00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:42.100
Muse Wonder reviews those structured findings alongside building records, helping the team focus its next

20
00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:43.110
survey.

21
00:01:44.072 --> 00:01:49.722
DINOv2 compares the 2020 and 2024 images locally in the browser.

22
00:01:49.752 --> 00:01:53.772
We can explore the highlighted locations in the original photographs.

23
00:01:54.655 --> 00:01:57.105
Muse Glimmer runs on the local computer.

24
00:01:57.135 --> 00:02:01.315
It connects our planning note to building records and owner requests.

25
00:02:01.455 --> 00:02:05.425
The team needs twenty thousand square feet of usable space.

26
00:02:05.455 --> 00:02:10.995
Here, verifying usable space could change which building we inspect first.

27
00:02:11.988 --> 00:02:19.078
Muse Image turns the written checks into an illustrated inspection plan, making the next steps easier to share

28
00:02:19.108 --> 00:02:20.808
with the project team.

29
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:29.270
Built during this hackathon, GEONOS brings more than a million mapped NYC building outlines into one focused

30
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.600
workspace.

31
00:02:31.572 --> 00:02:35.222
We started with two buildings and a practical question.

32
00:02:35.252 --> 00:02:42.022
Now we have a sourced comparison, a focused inspection plan, and clear requests for the owner.

33
00:02:42.052 --> 00:02:45.462
Export the evidence and project conditions.

34
00:02:45.492 --> 00:02:52.072
Seven Meta model roles work together to help us make the next building decision with better evidence.
